From: =?UTF-8?q?=D0=90=D0=BD=D0=B4=D1=80=D0=B5=D0=B9=20=D0=9C=D0=B0=D1=80?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=D0=BA=D0=B8=D0=BD?= <py6on@yandex.ru>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:59:38 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: t1100: enable Type-C PD on the MT6370 port

The TCPC node was disabled and the connector carried pd-disable, so the
port never negotiated USB PD and the charger stayed on the BC1.2 default
(5 V, IAICR 500 mA).

Enable the node, drop pd-disable, keep typec-power-opmode as a guard for
the non-PD path and declare both contracts the sink accepts (5 V/3 A and
9 V/2 A) with op-sink-microwatt = 18 W, which is what makes an 18 W PD
charger offer 9 V. No graph endpoints are added: tcpm looks the USB role
switch up through the connector's fwnode, and without a link it does not
drive mtu3, so the RNDIS gadget (the only SSH channel) survives.

PD does not raise the charge current by itself: mt6370-tcpc has no
set_current_limit() callback, tcpm only records the contract in
tcpm-source-psy-*; userspace applies it (t1100-charge.sh pd).
---
 .../boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts  | 41 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
index f6770dda4..232d96673 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6771-fplus-t1100.dts
@@ -392,21 +392,34 @@ charger {
 		 * switch nobody registers. Neither is needed to charge: the
 		 * charger above is an independent BC1.2 engine.
 		 *
-		 * pd-disable keeps the PD state machine out of the picture
-		 * for the first bring-up; sink-pdos documents the single 5 V
-		 * contract the port accepts.
+		 * PD is on: pd-disable is gone, so tcpm runs the PD state
+		 * machine. Two things matter for our USB network. First, the
+		 * role-switch paragraph above still holds and the connector
+		 * must not grow graph endpoints now that PD is enabled:
+		 * otherwise tcpm would drive mtu3's role on a power-role
+		 * change and the RNDIS gadget, our only SSH channel, would
+		 * die. Second, sink-pdos has to list every voltage we want to
+		 * accept: the contract is picked by matching the sink PDOs
+		 * against the source PDOs (tcpm_pd_select_pdo), so a single
+		 * 5 V entry keeps the port at 5 V no matter what the charger
+		 * offers. 9 V/2 A plus op-sink-microwatt = 18 W is what makes
+		 * an 18 W PD charger switch to 9 V.
+		 *
+		 * typec-power-opmode is only read on the non-PD path
+		 * (tcpm_fw_get_caps requires it when pd-disable is set), so it
+		 * stays as a guard in case PD gets disabled again: without it
+		 * probe fails with -EINVAL and -22.
+		 *
+		 * PD does not raise the charge current by itself: mt6370-tcpc
+		 * has no set_current_limit() callback, so tcpm only records
+		 * the contract in tcpm-source-psy-* and the charger keeps its
+		 * IAICR. Userspace applies the contract
+		 * (usr/local/bin/t1100-charge.sh pd).
 		 */
 		tcpc {
 			compatible = "mediatek,mt6370-tcpc";
 			interrupts-extended = <&pio 41 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW>;
-			/*
-			 * tcpci_parse_config() wants the child node named
-			 * "connector"; with the MFD-created platform device it is
-			 * not found and probe fails with -EINVAL. Charging does
-			 * not depend on the TCPC, so keep it off until the Type-C
-			 * role work (OTG/host) is on the table.
-			 */
-			status = "disabled";
+			status = "okay";
 
 			connector {
 				compatible = "usb-c-connector";
@@ -414,10 +427,12 @@ connector {
 
 				power-role = "sink";
 				data-role = "device";
-				pd-disable;
-				op-sink-microwatt = <15000000>;
+				typec-power-opmode = "default";
+				op-sink-microwatt = <18000000>;
 
 				sink-pdos = <PDO_FIXED(5000, 3000,
+						       PDO_FIXED_USB_COMM)
+					     PDO_FIXED(9000, 2000,
 						       PDO_FIXED_USB_COMM)>;
 			};
 		};
